# Shell scripts must be checked out with LF endings on every platform.
#
# These run under bash on Linux and macOS, and the first-boot script is run by
# bash on the Pi. A CRLF checkout makes the kernel look for an interpreter
# called "/bin/bash\r", which fails as "bad interpreter: No such file or
# directory" — and on a headless first boot there is nobody to read that.
#
# A Windows clone with core.autocrlf=true already stores LF in the index, so
# this is what keeps a clone configured otherwise from committing CRLF and
# breaking the Linux path.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The DietPi image is binary; never let Git translate anything inside it.
*.img binary
*.img.xz binary
